messages:
mina: Quick sync — we need to pick a pattern for internal APIs: typed-edge (many small HTTP endpoints with protobuf payloads and generated clients) vs a single gateway that does JSON<->proto transcoding. We care about rollout UX, telemetry attribution, and quotas. Any strong opinions?

liam: typed-edge for internal services + proto-first. Easier to reason about contract changes and canary targeting. generates clients = fewer integration bugs.

jules: +1 for contract safety but mobile/web teams want JSON. Gateway transcoding would be required either way. My worry: duplicated schemas and drift between gateway mapping and service protos. How do we version gracefully?

devrel-sam: From SDKs perspective auto-generated clients from proto defs is huge. But we must make release flow smooth — if every proto bump requires coord with app teams that's slower. Can we support stable JSON DTOs generated from protos?

ops-bot: deploy-bot: note: last gateway deploy showed 200ms p99 regression traced to the transcoding layer (added 3ms per-request CPU decode/encode on high payloads).

mina: Proposal: proto-first contract, expose two access paths: 1) typed-edge for internal backend-to-backend traffic (grpc/http2 + proto) which supports precise quotas and backpressure; 2) public gateway that transcodes for SDKs/apps. Keep schema single source of truth (protos) and generate both. That keeps UX wins while limiting infra complexity. Thoughts on telemetry & quotas?

liam: For telemetry we need stable attributes regardless of path. Suggest we inject headers at the edge: `X-Redwood-API: service=Predict;version=v2` and `X-Proto-Fingerprint: <sha256>` so traces/metrics can correlate across transcoding boundary. Also add `x-rw-client-kind: sdk|internal` for quota bucketing.

jules: Quotas: gateway-level coarse quotas are fine for public tier, but for per-customer/model billing we need enforcement closer to compute — typed-edge allows per-model token accounting before request fanout. Otherwise gateway has to be stateful and maintain expensive counters.

devrel-sam: Rollouts UX: support traffic-splitting via header-based routing and cookie/sticky hashes. Example test header for canary: `curl -H 'X-Canary-Group: beta-users' -d '{..}' https://api.redwood.ai/predict` We should standardize a small set of routing headers in the platform.

mina: Quick examples to align. Proto service snippet:```proto
service Predictor {
  rpc Predict(PredictRequest) returns (PredictResponse) {}
}
message PredictRequest {
  string model = 1;
  bytes prompt = 2;
}
``` JSON request that gateway would accept:```json
{
  "model": "redwood-v1",
  "prompt": "summarize this..."
}
``` Gateway should add headers before calling typed-edge: `X-Redwood-API: service=Predict;version=v2` and `X-Proto-Fingerprint: abc123`

liam: Sounds reasonable. Next step: write a short RFC that outlines the two-path approach, header conventions, quota enforcement boundaries, and rollout header tooling. I can draft the telemetry/trace mapping section.

mina: Perfect. I'll open the RFC and tag you both. Thanks — let's keep the gateway transcoding but treat typed-edges as first-class for infra-sensitive flows. :thumbsup:
